## The Numbers Speak

Total recalls this year have climbed to 327. Out of these, 90 were classified as Class I, meaning that the products posed a serious health risk. That's an average of nearly 13 recalls every month--an unsettling figure for anyone who assumes their groceries are safe.

## Repeat Offenders

The data reveals that 67 brands have had repeat recalls this year. Leading the charge is CHETAK NEW YORK LLC, with a staggering 52 recalls, all Class I. If you've seen their products on shelves, you might want to think twice.

## The Culprits

Recent recalls have highlighted an alarming range of issues, from undeclared allergens to contamination with Salmonella. For example:

- Unistel Industries Inc. recalled its Ground All Spice due to potential Salmonella contamination.
- Costco Wholesale Corporation's Kirkland Signature Madeleines were found to contain undeclared hazelnut and soy lecithin.
- Ethnic Foods Inc. had to pull products for containing undeclared artificial food coloring.

These are just a few examples from the most recent spate of recalls.

## Worst Offenders

The worst brands by recall performance include:

- Moonlight Packing Corporation
- Albertsons Companies LLC
- CHETAK NEW YORK LLC

## What This Means For You

The numbers clearly show that food recalls are not slowing down. As consumers, staying informed is crucial to avoid potential health hazards. Always check for recall notices and be aware of the brands and products that are frequently flagged.

## The Monthly Breakdown

Interestingly, the recall rates have fluctuated, with October 2025 seeing a spike at 146 recalls. April 2026 saw 84 recalls, showing a slight decline but still significant.

## Verdict

The recall data is a reminder of the importance of transparency and accountability in the food industry. Consumers should support brands that prioritize safety and push for stricter regulations in food production.
